rogrammers-guide/api-getting-started.html#example">Example</a></li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2 id=3D"introduction">Introduction</h2>
<p>The OnBase Document Management API is a Representational State Transfer,=
 or REST, API offering programmatic access to an OnBase database. It includ=
es endpoints for various OnBase activities including, but not limited to, v=
iewing and uploading Document content and Document metadata, viewing and re=
placing Keyword data on a Document, executing Custom Queries, and retrievin=
g various OnBase configuration items, such as Document Types, Keyword Types=
, File Types, and others. </p>
<h2 id=3D"api-server">API Server</h2>
<p>The Document Management API lives on the OnBase API Server. It is a home=
 for RESTful services for customers desiring a more traditional deployment.=
 Most configuration of the Document Management API will take place under th=
e OnBase API Server. For more information about the OnBase API Server, see =
the <a href=3D"https://support.hyland.com/r/OnBase/API-Server/Foundation-25=
.1/API-Server/Overview">OnBase API Server Module Reference Guide (MRG)</a>.=
</p>
<h2 id=3D"identity-provider">Identity Provider</h2>
<p>The Document Management API requires an instance of the Hyland Identity =
Provider or IdP. The Hyland IdP will authenticate and authorize the current=
 user. For more information on the Hyland IdP, see the <a href=3D"https://s=
upport.hyland.com/r/OnBase/Identity-and-Access-Management-Services/Foundati=
on-25.1/Integrating-With-Hyland-IAM-Services/Overview">Identity and Access =
Management Services Module Reference Guide (MRG)</a>.</p>
<h2 id=3D"temp-file-storage">Temp File Storage</h2>
<p>The Document Management API uses a temp file storage solution for upload=
ing Documents and retrieving partial content of Documents. The OnBase API S=
erver comes with a temp file storage solution that will work with the Docum=
ent Management API. For more information about the Temp File Service API, s=
ee the <a href=3D"https://support.hyland.com/r/OnBase/API-Server/Foundation=
-25.1/API-Server/Overview">OnBase API Server Module Reference Guide (MRG)</=
a>.</p>
<h2 id=3D"database">Database</h2>
<p>The Document Management API requires an OnBase Database. It requires no =
extra database set up or configuration. </p>
<h2 id=3D"scim">SCIM</h2>
<p>The Hyland SCIM (System for Cross-domain Identity Management) server is =
required to use the Hyland IdP to authenticate users in OnBase. It is insta=
lled as part of the API Server. For more information about the Hyland SCIM =
server, see the <a href=3D"https://support.hyland.com/r/OnBase/Identity-and=
-Access-Management-Services/Foundation-25.1/Integrating-With-Hyland-IAM-Ser=
vices/Overview">Identity and Access Management Services Module Reference Gu=
id (MRG)</a>.</p>
<h2 id=3D"configuration">Configuration</h2>
<p>There are several configuration files in the API Server that are utilize=
d by the Document Management API. These can be found in the config folder o=
f the API Server. First is 1_server.json. There are items for the IdP addre=
ss, the address for the Temp File Service, and the tenant. Next is 5_docume=
nt_management.json. It has two options: AllowedOrigins and IdP Scope Name. =
Allowed Origins is a semicolon separated list of the origins allowed for cr=
oss-origin resource sharing, or CORS. It is optional. If no list is supplie=
d, no cross-origin resource sharing will be allowed. The IdP Scope Name is =
the name of the scope of the API resources that a client can access from. S=
copes are configured on the IdP and this name needs to match one on the IDP=
 being used. It is required. Finally, there is 5_tempfs.json. There are opt=
ions for the IdP Scope Name and the tenant for the Temp FIle Service. This =
is different from the tenant for the server as a whole. For more informatio=
n about these items and configuring the API Server in general, see the <a h=
ref=3D"https://support.hyland.com/r/OnBase/API-Server/Foundation-25.1/API-S=
erver/Overview">OnBase API Server Module Reference Guide (MRG)</a>.</p>
<h2 id=3D"logging">Logging</h2>
<p>Logging in the Document Management API is quite flexible. It can be rout=
ed to several different services with a configurable minimum level. Logging=
 is built around Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.ILogger and Serilog so it can=
 log to the ELK stack ("ELK" is the acronym for three open source projects:=
 Elasticsearch, Logstash, and Kibana), or file (among others) as well as th=
e Hyland Diagnostics Console where logs will appear under the API Server ta=
b. Logging can be configured through the API Server in the configuration fi=
le 0_logging.json. Out-of-the-box, there should already be a route for conf=
igured for the Diagnostics Console and example, commented-out routes for wr=
iting to Console and File.</p>
<h2 id=3D"example">Example</h2>
<p>Here's a diagram showing how the pieces work together. This is for a req=
uest for a Document content range, to show the Temp File Service.<br><img s=
rc=3D"https://sdk.onbase.com/rest/OnBase/Foundation26.1/document/programmer=
s-guide/images/getting-started.png" alt=3D"Document-Management-API-Getting-=
Started"></p>
